# Per-package statement-coverage floors, checked in CI by scripts/coverfloor.sh.
# A floor never goes down: when a package gains coverage, raise its floor in the same change.
# internal/eval is the evaluation harness itself; it is proven by running the tiers, not by unit
# coverage, so its floor is deliberately low and rises as its pure logic gets tests.
#
# internal/vm was lowered from 79 to 55 when the second, third and fourth backends landed, and
# that is a deliberate weakening rather than a drift. The package now holds four drivers, and two
# of them — Apple's `container` and any OCI daemon — can only be exercised where that runtime is
# installed. Measured on this package: ~80% with every runtime present, 71% with docker and podman
# but no Apple container, 55% with none, which is what CI sees. A floor of 79 would have meant CI
# failing for a reason that has nothing to do with the change under test.
#
# The coverage lost is not unverified: each driver is checked end to end by `make smoke` against a
# real backend (BOXER_BACKEND=podman make smoke), which is a stronger test than a unit percentage,
# and the parts that can be tested anywhere — decoding, error classification, capabilities, the
# refusals — are covered unconditionally. Raised to 65 on 2026-09-28, when a fake `container` CLI
# brought the Apple driver under test on every host: CI measured 67.6% on macOS, 78.7% on Linux.
internal/box       82
internal/bundle    87
internal/config    66
internal/cli       73
internal/decide    99
internal/eval      14
internal/fakellm   81
internal/hook      90
internal/inside    92
internal/install   84
internal/junit     87
internal/mcp       94
internal/obs       81
internal/scope     87
internal/shim      67
internal/vm        65
pkg/boxer          78
cmd/boxer          78
